/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The bilateral (two-sided) Laplace transform of a signal x(t) integrates over all time:
   - x(t) is the time signal; s = σ + jω is the complex frequency; σ = Re(s) is the damping in s⁻¹; ω = Im(s) is the angular frequency in rad/s; X(s) is the transform.
   - Split the kernel: e−st = e−σt·e−jωt.
   - The unilateral (one-sided) transform starts at t = 0⁻, just before zero: X(s) = ∫0⁻∞ x(t)e−stdt.
   - Take the right-sided exponential x(t) = e−atu(t), with a real.
   - The upper limit gives zero only if e−(s+a)t → 0 as t → ∞, that is Re(s + a) > 0.
   - Now the left-sided signal x(t) = −e−atu(−t), which is nonzero only for t < 0.
   - So one algebraic expression, 1/(s + a), belongs to two different signals.
   - Convergence depends only on σ = Re(s), because |e−st| = e−σt.
   - Figure 4.1.1 draws the three cases for a = 2: the right half-plane, the left half-plane and, for the two-sided signal of the worked example, the strip between −2 and 3.
*/
