/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In the table, x(t) ↔ X(s) with ROC R, and x₁(t) ↔ X₁(s) with ROC R₁, x₂(t) ↔ X₂(s) with ROC R₂.
   - 'Contains' means the ROC can be larger, when a pole cancels.
   - The shift in s is also called frequency shifting.
   - The one-sided transform is used for systems that start at t = 0 with stored energy.
   - The upper limit gives 0 inside the ROC. x(0⁻) is the value just before t = 0, for example the voltage on a capacitor when a switch closes.
   - x′(0⁻) is the slope just before t = 0.
   - Two more rules complete the set.
   - x(0⁺) is the value just after t = 0.
   - Valid only when every pole of sX(s) lies strictly in the left half-plane, Re(s) < 0.
   - Both theorems are for one-sided transforms of signals that start at t = 0.
*/
